messages:
kevin-docs: Opening a small docs PR to recommend a stricter structured output pattern for function/tool returns. Looking for quick reviews, a JSON Schema snippet we can include, and two tiny SDK normalize examples (JS + Py).

anya-ml: +1. Please call out the edge cases: single object vs array, omitted optional keys vs explicit null, and union item discrimination.

kevin-docs: Draft guidance in PR body: 1) Top-level `response` object with `kind`, `version`, `data` (always array). 2) `meta` for provenance. 3) Prefer explicit nulls for optional fields. 4) If items are unions, include a `type` discriminator on each item.

marc-devex: We'll need backwards-compatible parsing snippets for SDKs. Keep the shim tiny and paste-friendly. Also add a unit-test suggestion that normalizers always return `data` as an array.

julia-ux: Add a before/after diff and a one-liner on why arrays are better (predictable iteration and batching). People copy examples straight into apps.

kevin-docs: Draft before/after: 
```json
// OLD ambiguous
{ "answer": { "text": "ok" } }

// NEW recommended
{
  "response": {
    "kind": "tool_response",
    "version": "1.0",
    "data": [ { "text": "ok" } ],
    "meta": { "model": "redwood-v1", "timestamp": 1710000000 }
  }
}
```

anya-ml: For nullable example show `data: [{"note": null}]` and explain why that's easier for typed clients than omitting `note`.

marc-devex: JS normalize snippet for the SDK docs (very small):
```js
function normalize(resp) {
  if (resp && resp.response && Array.isArray(resp.response.data)) return resp.response;
  if (Array.isArray(resp)) return { kind: 'legacy_array', version: '0', data: resp };
  if (resp && resp.answer) return { kind: 'legacy_answer', version: '0', data: Array.isArray(resp.answer) ? resp.answer : [resp.answer] };
  return { kind: 'unknown', version: '0', data: [] };
}
```

kevin-docs: Python equivalent: 
```py
def normalize(resp):
    if resp and isinstance(resp.get('response'), dict) and isinstance(resp['response'].get('data'), list):
        return resp['response']
    if isinstance(resp, list):
        return {'kind': 'legacy_array', 'version': '0', 'data': resp}
    if resp and 'answer' in resp:
        r = resp['answer']
        return {'kind': 'legacy_answer', 'version': '0', 'data': r if isinstance(r, list) else [r]}
    return {'kind': 'unknown', 'version': '0', 'data': []}
```

julia-ux: Nice. Add a consumer snippet: `for item in normalized['data']:` and a short note: "If item has `type`, switch on it."

kevin-docs: Adding a compact JSON Schema for CI-example validation in the PR: `response` required, `data` array of objects allowed, `meta` optional. That will catch malformed examples (missing commas, etc.).

anya-ml: Also add guidance for union discriminators: show an example item `{ "type": "file", "path": "/tmp/x" }` and recommend `meta.discriminator` only if needed.

marc-devex: I'll add a TODO in the SDK repo to include these normalize helpers and a unit test asserting `data` is an array. We'll also grep the docs for old `answer`/`result` shapes and schedule targeted edits rather than a big replace.

ci-bot: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/5299 - "Structured response envelope + SDK normalize shims" by kevin-docs. Docs build passed (examples schema check enabled).

kevin-docs: Thanks all. If no more comments I'll merge tomorrow and follow up with a small set of targeted example updates and link-fixes. Tagging @marc-devex and @anya-ml for final signoff. :rocket:
